<clickhouse>
    <!--
      Per-query introspection: OFF by default, available on demand.

      `config.d/logging.xml` bounds how long the system logs are KEPT. A TTL
      does nothing about the cost of WRITING them, and two of those tables are
      written for every query and read by nobody:

        system.processors_profile_log   391.7M rows/day    29.6 GiB
        system.trace_log                 36.9M rows/day     8.3 GiB

      Measured 2026-09-18: zero SELECTs against either table in the 14 days
      query_log retains, against 36,828 SELECTs of query_log itself over the
      same window. The processor log is per PROCESSOR per query, so it is not
      proportional to work done — `SELECT count() FROM numbers(1000)` writes 42
      rows. At ~4.4M queries/day that is where the 391.7M comes from.

      Turning them off by default costs nothing for investigation, because both
      are ordinary settings: an actual debugging session asks for them per query
      or per session and gets the full detail back.

          SELECT … SETTINGS log_processors_profiles = 1
          SET query_profiler_real_time_period_ns = 1000000000   (1s sampling)

      That is the whole reason this is a default rather than a removal: the
      earlier decision to keep processor profiling "worth having during an
      investigation" still holds — it just should not be billed to all 4.4M
      queries a day to stay available.

      query_log stays ON. It is the one that earns its keep (every performance
      diagnosis on this deployment reads it) and its retention is a security
      property, not just a disk one — see the note in config.d/logging.xml.
    -->
    <profiles>
        <default>
            <log_processors_profiles>0</log_processors_profiles>
            <!-- Both profiler periods must be 0; either one alone still samples. -->
            <query_profiler_real_time_period_ns>0</query_profiler_real_time_period_ns>
            <query_profiler_cpu_time_period_ns>0</query_profiler_cpu_time_period_ns>
            <!--
              trace_log has more feeders than the two periods above. Measured
              over 3 minutes with those already at 0: Memory 19,007 +
              MemoryPeak 17,587 + Real 15,927 + CPU 307. The memory pair comes
              from the allocation profiler, which samples every
              memory_profiler_step bytes (4 MiB) per query thread and is a
              separate switch.

              The residue is written by threads that do not read this profile
              (background/merge work takes the SERVER defaults, notably
              total_memory_profiler_step). Turning that off needs config.d and
              therefore a ClickHouse restart, which costs the whole stack a
              multi-minute outage — not worth it for the remainder, so it is
              left running deliberately.
            -->
            <memory_profiler_step>0</memory_profiler_step>
            <memory_profiler_sample_probability>0</memory_profiler_sample_probability>
        </default>
    </profiles>
</clickhouse>
